{"authored" [{"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Find the coefficients $A$ and $B$ in its partial-fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\qquad x\\ne3,-\\frac12.$$\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}, $B=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/review.json" "file_sha256" "f5d1f6d7608bba3923bc4efd4d53f2051dd3e091e98bd5f6c249e7b20db11396" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "89292a344cd79eda20f8f6c8de6da8c16fdbffe463c7cb34c4e4684200328e95" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/verification.json" "basis" 1720 "rationale" "The original source separates a convergent rational-function improper integral into two divergent improper integrals, which is invalid. The requested partial-fraction coefficients are unique and the site actually graded A=1, B=-2 Correct. The authorized study correction asks directly for the partial-fraction decomposition and explains common finite-cutoff integration. Original source wording, source key, screenshots, grades, solver decisions and historical field/answer identities remain retained; new study fields and answers receive distinct immutable identities."} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Factor the denominator:\n\n$$2x^2-5x-3=(x-3)(2x+1).$$\n\nMultiplying the partial-fraction identity by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3)=(2A+B)x+(A-3B).$$\n\nMatching coefficients yields $2A+B=0$ and $A-3B=7$. Thus $B=-2A$, so $7A=7$ and\n\n$$A=1,\\qquad B=-2.$$\n\nSubstitution verifies the identity because $(2x+1)-2(x-3)=7$.\n\nIf this decomposition is used to evaluate the improper integral from $4$ to infinity, keep a common finite upper bound $R$ and take the limit only after combining the terms:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left(\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}\\right)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe two separate improper integrals each diverge, so they cannot be subtracted as independently evaluated improper integrals." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/review.json" "file_sha256" "f5d1f6d7608bba3923bc4efd4d53f2051dd3e091e98bd5f6c249e7b20db11396" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "89292a344cd79eda20f8f6c8de6da8c16fdbffe463c7cb34c4e4684200328e95" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/verification.json" "basis" 1720 "rationale" "The original source separates a convergent rational-function improper integral into two divergent improper integrals, which is invalid. The requested partial-fraction coefficients are unique and the site actually graded A=1, B=-2 Correct. The authorized study correction asks directly for the partial-fraction decomposition and explains common finite-cutoff integration. Original source wording, source key, screenshots, grades, solver decisions and historical field/answer identities remain retained; new study fields and answers receive distinct immutable identities."} {"question" "q-128339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/review.json" "file_sha256" "f5d1f6d7608bba3923bc4efd4d53f2051dd3e091e98bd5f6c249e7b20db11396" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "89292a344cd79eda20f8f6c8de6da8c16fdbffe463c7cb34c4e4684200328e95" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/verification.json" "basis" 1720 "rationale" "The original source separates a convergent rational-function improper integral into two divergent improper integrals, which is invalid. The requested partial-fraction coefficients are unique and the site actually graded A=1, B=-2 Correct. The authorized study correction asks directly for the partial-fraction decomposition and explains common finite-cutoff integration. Original source wording, source key, screenshots, grades, solver decisions and historical field/answer identities remain retained; new study fields and answers receive distinct immutable identities."} {"question" "q-128339" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/review.json" "file_sha256" "f5d1f6d7608bba3923bc4efd4d53f2051dd3e091e98bd5f6c249e7b20db11396" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "89292a344cd79eda20f8f6c8de6da8c16fdbffe463c7cb34c4e4684200328e95" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128339/verification.json" "basis" 1720 "rationale" "The original source separates a convergent rational-function improper integral into two divergent improper integrals, which is invalid. The requested partial-fraction coefficients are unique and the site actually graded A=1, B=-2 Correct. The authorized study correction asks directly for the partial-fraction decomposition and explains common finite-cutoff integration. Original source wording, source key, screenshots, grades, solver decisions and historical field/answer identities remain retained; new study fields and answers receive distinct immutable identities."} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d948f0af41942ad914fe02baa56ce191773938ec425e295f7cf2cf4abca19" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "680d948f0af41942ad914fe02baa56ce191773938ec425e295f7cf2cf4abca19" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-128339" []} "basis" 1720 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186327826 17592186327828] "immutable_fields" [[17592186327825 :answer-field/correct] [17592186327825 :answer-field/id] [17592186327825 :answer-field/key] [17592186327825 :answer-field/type] [17592186327827 :answer-field/correct] [17592186327827 :answer-field/id] [17592186327827 :answer-field/key] [17592186327827 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
